### Short answer  
**It’s not a bug.**  
When a style **doesn’t have a `glyphs` URL**, MapLibre GL JS (v5.11 + ) falls back to **“local-fonts mode”**.  
Each `text-font` entry is treated exactly like a CSS `font-family` list:

1. The browser tries to find a **system font** that matches the name.  
2. If you have added a **web-font** with `@font-face` (or via the style’s `font-faces` property and `map.setFontFaces()`), the browser will load that file and use it.  
3. If the name can’t be resolved to any installed or loaded font, the browser picks the **default generic font** (usually `sans-serif`), which is why you see a “generic” look.

So the map is working as designed; the only thing missing is a **real font that the browser can actually use**.

---

## What’s really happening under the hood

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **1. Style parsing** | The style JSON is read. No `glyphs` property → MapLibre knows it must use the *local-fonts* code path. |
| **2. Symbol layout** | For each `symbol` layer the `text-font` array is read. The array is interpreted as a **fallback list** just like CSS (`["My Fancy Font", "Noto Sans Regular", "sans-serif"]`). |
| **3. Font resolution** | MapLibre asks the browser’s text engine to **measure & rasterise** the text. The browser looks for a font that matches the first name; if it can’t find one it goes to the next name, and finally to the generic family. |
| **4. Rendering** | The browser returns an **SDF bitmap** (generated on-the-fly by TinySDF) for each glyph. MapLibre draws those bitmaps on the map. No glyph-PBF request is ever made because there is no `glyphs` URL. |
| **5. Fallback logging** | If a name can’t be resolved you’ll see a console warning like: <br>`[maplibre-gl] Unable to find local font "My Fancy Font", falling back to system font.` (only in dev builds). |

Because the whole process is delegated to the browser, **the result depends on what fonts are installed on the user’s device or what web-fonts you have loaded**. That’s why you see a generic font on machines that don’t have the custom font installed.

---

## How to make the intended font appear

### 1. Load the font as a **web-font** (recommended)

```css
/* In your page’s CSS */
@font-face {
  font-family: "My Fancy Font";
  src: url("https://example.com/fonts/MyFancyFont-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: 400;
  font-style: normal;
}
```

*or* use the **style-level `font-faces` property** (available in GL JS 6.7 +):

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "My Fancy Font": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyFancyFont-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-FFFF"]   // optional, can limit to needed blocks
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Then, **after the map is created**, tell MapLibre about the new `font-faces` (if you added them at runtime):

```js
map.setFontFaces({
  "My Fancy Font": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyFancyFont-Regular.ttf"
    }
  ]
});
```

*Make sure*:

* The URL is **CORS-enabled** (the server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your site’s origin).  
* The font file format is supported by the browser (`.woff2` is best, `.ttf`/`.otf` also work).  
* The **font-family name** in `@font-face`/`font-faces` **exactly matches** the string you use in `text-font`.

### 2. Use a **system-installed font**

If you want to rely on fonts that are already present on the user’s OS (e.g., `"Arial"` or `"Noto Sans"`), make sure the name you write in `text-font` matches the OS-registered family name. Keep in mind that the exact appearance will vary between Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, Android, etc.

### 3. (Optional) Keep a **glyphs server** for cross-platform consistency

If you need **identical rendering on every device** (including MapLibre Native, which cannot fall back to local fonts), add a `glyphs` URL that points at a glyph-PBF server (e.g., the public demo server or a self-hosted copy of the OpenMapTiles font set). Then the style will use the same SDF glyphs everywhere, and you can still load extra web-fonts via `font-faces` for scripts that need shaping (Arabic, Devanagari, etc.).

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://my-tileserver.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["My Fancy Font", "Noto Sans Regular"]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### 4. Special cases – scripts that need shaping

* **Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Thai, Khmer, etc.** – Even when `glyphs` is present, GL JS ≥ 6.7 draws these scripts using the **browser’s shaping engine** (or HarfBuzz on Native). Therefore you **must** provide the appropriate font file via `font-faces` (or load it as a web-font) so the browser can find a font that contains the required glyphs and OpenType tables.

```json
"font-faces": {
  "Noto Sans Arabic": [
    {
      "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
    }
  ]
}
```

* **CJK (Chinese/Japanese/Korean)** – By default GL JS uses `localIdeographFontFamily` (default `'sans-serif'`) and **generates the glyphs locally** with TinySDF, ignoring `text-font` for those characters. This is intentional to avoid dozens of large glyph-PBF requests per tile. If you *really* want to serve CJK glyph PBFs, set `localIdeographFontFamily: false` in the map options, but expect a performance hit.

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",
  style: "style.json",
  localIdeographFontFamily: false   // serve CJK glyphs from the glyphs URL
});
```

---

## TL;DR checklist

| ✅ | What to verify / do |
|---|----------------------|
| **1** | Your style **does not** have a `glyphs` property → GL JS is using local-fonts mode (expected). |
| **2** | The font name you put in `text-font` **matches** a font that the browser can actually load (system font **or** a web-font you added). |
| **3** | If you’re using a custom font, add it via **`@font-face`** *or* the **`font-faces`** property + `map.setFontFaces()`. |
| **4** | Ensure the font file URL is CORS-enabled and the file format is browser-compatible. |
| **5** (optional) | If you need identical rendering on Native or want to avoid reliance on local fonts, **add a `glyphs` URL** that serves the same font-stack as your `text-font` list. |
| **6** (script-specific) | For Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Thai, etc., provide the appropriate font via `font-faces`; GL JS will shape the text with the browser engine. |
| **7** (CJK) | Leave `localIdeographFontFamily` at its default (`'sans-serif'`) unless you have a strong reason to serve CJK glyph PBFs. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to spot them

| Symptom | Likely cause | How to confirm |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| Labels appear, but **different** font on different OSes | Using a **system-font name** that isn’t installed on all machines. | Open the browser’s dev-tools → **Computed Style** for a label (inspect the canvas element, enable “Show paint rectangles” → you’ll see the font family used). |
| No text at all (or missing characters) | Font file **fails to load** (404, CORS, unsupported format). | Check the network tab for failed font requests; look for `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` errors. |
| Arabic text shows as **disconnected letters** (glyphs not joined) | Using a **glyph-PBF only** without a `font-faces` entry (pre-6.7 GL JS). | Upgrade to GL JS ≥ 6.7 or add `font-faces` with a proper Arabic font. |
| CJK characters look **pixelated** or **slow to appear** | `localIdeographFontFamily` set to `false` (forcing many glyph-PBF fetches). | Remove that option or set it back to a font family name. |
| Font appears on desktop but **not on mobile** | Mobile browser cannot fetch the web-font (CORS, mixed-content, or unsupported format). | Test on the device, look at network logs, try `.woff2` (most widely supported). |

---

## TL;DR code example (complete)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom local font</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    @font-face {
      font-family: "My Fancy Font";
      src: url("https://example.com/fonts/MyFancyFont-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: normal;
    }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "sources": {
        "osm": {
          "type": "raster",
          "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          "tileSize": 256
        }
      },
      "layers": [
        {
          "id": "osm",
          "type": "raster",
          "source": "osm"
        },
        {
          "id": "city-labels",
          "type": "symbol",
          "source": "osm",
          "layout": {
            "text-field": "My City",
            "text-font": ["My Fancy Font", "Noto Sans Regular"],
            "text-size": 24,
            "text-anchor": "center"
          },
          "paint": {
            "text-color": "#000"
          }
        }
      ]
      // No `glyphs` property → local-fonts mode
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // If you prefer to use the style-level `font-faces` instead of CSS:
  // map.setFontFaces({
  //   "My Fancy Font": [{ url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyFancyFont-Regular.ttf" }]
  // });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Running the above will render “My City” using **My Fancy Font** on every device that can fetch the `.woff2` file. If the font fails to load, the browser falls back to the next entry (`Noto Sans Regular`) and finally to the generic `sans-serif` – exactly what you observed.

---

### Bottom line
- **No `glyphs` → local-fonts mode** (expected).  
- The generic font you see means the browser **couldn’t find the font you named**.  
- Load the font via `@font-face` or `font-faces` (and optionally `map.setFontFaces`) and the map will use it.  
- Keep a `glyphs` URL only if you need identical rendering on MapLibre Native or you want to avoid reliance on the client’s installed fonts.

## Short answer  
Yes – the **same `font-faces` block you use in a MapLibre Native style can be copied into a MapLibre GL JS style** and you will get the same “offline-font” behaviour, **provided** you meet a few GL JS-specific requirements:

| Requirement | What you have to do |
|-------------|---------------------|
| **GL JS version** | Use **≥ 6.7.0** (the version that first reads `font-faces`). 6.9.0+ also handles Arabic/Hebrew shaping, and 6.15.0+ draws complex scripts entirely from the local font. |
| **Reachable font files** | The URLs in `font-faces` must be fetchable by the browser (http/https, same-origin or CORS-enabled). If you truly need *offline* use, pre-cache them with a Service Worker or bundle them as data-URIs. |
| **Optional runtime update** | If you want to change the mapping after the map is created, call `map.setFontFaces()` with the same object (or a modified one). |
| **Glyphs URL** | You can **omit** the `glyphs` field entirely (GL JS will fall back to the local-font path for every `text-font`). If you keep a `glyphs` URL, GL JS will still try to fetch PBFs for any code-point that is **not** covered by a `unicode-range` in a `font-faces` entry. |
| **CJK handling** | CJK characters are still rendered by the TinySDF “local-ideograph” path unless you set `localIdeographFontFamily: false`. The `font-faces` entry for a CJK font will be ignored for those code-points. |

Below is a step-by-step checklist and a minimal example that mirrors a typical Native `font-faces` config.

---

## 1️⃣ Make sure you are on a recent GL JS build  

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

If you are using a bundler, import the same version:

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
```

> **Why it matters** – `font-faces` was added in 6.7.0. Earlier versions simply ignore the property and fall back to the glyph-PBF server (or fail if `glyphs` is missing).

---

## 2️⃣ Copy the `font-faces` block verbatim  

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // optional; can be omitted
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]   // Latin + Latin-Extended-A
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ]
}
```

*Everything that works in Native works here* – the keys (`url`, `unicode-range`) are identical.

### What changes for the browser?

* **`url` must be reachable by the browser** – either a relative path (`fonts/...`) that you serve from the same origin, or an absolute URL that has proper CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain).  
* If you want true offline use, **pre-cache** those `.ttf/.otf` files with a Service Worker or bundle them as base-64 data URIs. Example Service Worker snippet:

```js
self.addEventListener('install', e => {
  e.waitUntil(
    caches.open('font-cache').then(cache => {
      return cache.addAll([
        '/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf',
        '/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf',
        '/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf'
      ]);
    })
  );
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', e => {
  if (e.request.destination === 'font') {
    e.respondWith(
      caches.match(e.request).then(resp => resp || fetch(e.request))
    );
  }
});
```

Now the browser can load the fonts even when the network is down.

---

## 3️⃣ (Optional) Call `map.setFontFaces()` at runtime  

If you load the style via `map.setStyle()` **after** the map has been created, you can inject the same `font-faces` object without editing the JSON file:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style.json',          // may omit `font-faces` here
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  const fontFaces = {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      { url: "fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf", "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"] }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari Regular": [
      { url: "fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf", "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"] }
    ]
  };
  map.setFontFaces(fontFaces);
});
```

> **Why you might need this** – When you bundle the style in a JS string (e.g., generated on the fly) or when you want to swap a font set for a different language without re-loading the whole style.

---

## 4️⃣ Decide what to do with the `glyphs` field  

### Option A – **Omit `glyphs` completely**  

```json
{
  "glyphs": null,
  "font-faces": { … }
}
```

*GL JS ≥ 5.11.0* treats a missing `glyphs` URL as “local-fonts mode”. Every `text-font` name is resolved exactly like a CSS `font-family` list: the browser loads the `@font-face` files you declared in `font-faces`. No PBF requests are made, so the app works fully offline (as long as the font files themselves are cached).

### Option B – **Keep a `glyphs` URL**  

If you keep a `glyphs` URL (e.g., for fallback Latin glyphs you don’t want to bundle), GL JS will still **prefer** the `font-faces` entry for any code-point that falls inside a declared `unicode-range`. Only characters **outside** all declared ranges will trigger a glyph-PBF request. This is useful when you have a small subset of fonts offline but still want the rest of the world covered by a remote glyph server.

> **CJK note** – Even with a `glyphs` URL, CJK characters are *never* taken from the PBFs; they are always generated locally via `localIdeographFontFamily`. If you really need the exact shapes from a specific CJK font, set `localIdeographFontFamily: false` in the map options and serve a CJK font via `font-faces`. This is rarely needed because the on-device fonts are already high-quality.

---

## 5️⃣ Test it  

1. **Open DevTools → Network** and filter by “font”. You should see only the `.ttf/.otf` requests you listed in `font-faces`. No `*.pbf` requests (unless you kept a `glyphs` URL and the text contains characters outside your ranges).  
2. **Turn off the network** (Chrome DevTools → Offline) and refresh. The map should still render all labels that belong to the ranges you declared.  
3. **Check console** – GL JS logs a warning if a `unicode-range` is missing for a code-point and it falls back to the glyph server:  

   ```
   Unable to load glyph range ... Rendering codepoint U+XXXX locally instead.
   ```

   If you see this, either add the missing range to a `font-faces` entry or accept the fallback.

---

## 6️⃣ Gotchas & pitfalls  

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **CORS errors** when loading fonts from another domain | Browsers block cross-origin font loads without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. | Host the fonts on the same origin, or configure the remote server to send the header. |
| **Fonts not applied** – text still shows default system font | The `text-font` name in the style does **not** exactly match the key in `font-faces`. | Ensure the string is identical (including case and spaces). |
| **Missing characters** – some glyphs appear as tofu | The character lies outside any `unicode-range` you declared. | Add the appropriate range (or a broader range) to the relevant entry. |
| **Large bundle size** – you bundled many TTFs and the page becomes heavy | Each TTF can be several MB. | Use sub-setting: only include the Unicode blocks you actually need, or keep the fonts on a CDN and rely on the browser cache. |
| **RTL text still looks wrong** (pre-6.9.0) | GL JS before 6.9.0 needs the RTL plugin. | Upgrade to ≥ 6.9.0, or load `setRTLTextPlugin` as a fallback for older builds. |
| **CJK still looks odd** – you expected your custom CJK font to be used | `localIdeographFontFamily` is still set to the default `'sans-serif'`. | Either set `localIdeographFontFamily: '"Your CJK Font", sans-serif'` **or** disable the local-ideograph mode (`localIdeographFontFamily: false`) and provide a CJK entry in `font-faces`. |

---

## 7️⃣ TL;DR checklist for copying the Native `font-faces` to GL JS

1. **Upgrade GL JS to ≥ 6.7.0** (preferably the latest stable).  
2. **Copy the `font-faces` block** unchanged into your GL JS style JSON.  
3. **Make the font URLs reachable** by the browser (same-origin or CORS-enabled).  
4. **(Optional) Omit `glyphs`** if you want a pure offline experience.  
5. **(Optional) Cache the font files** with a Service Worker for true offline use.  
6. **Test offline** – verify no `.pbf` requests fire and all expected characters render.  

If you follow those steps, the GL JS map will render exactly the same way as your Native app, using the same local font files and Unicode-range filtering, even when the network is unavailable. 🎉
